3:T9a5,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"MedicalCondition","name":"Diabetic Retinopathy","description":"Diabetic retinopathy is a complication of diabetes (Type 1 and Type 2) in which high blood sugar levels damage the tiny blood vessels in the retina. It is one of the leading causes of preventable blindness in working-age adults in the UK. The condition often develops without any noticeable symptoms in its early stages, making the NHS Diabetic Eye Screening Programme — which invites everyone with diabetes for annual retinal photography — an essential safeguard.","url":"https://www.eyetest.co.uk/eye-health/conditions/diabetic-retinopathy","signOrSymptom":[{"@type":"MedicalSymptom","name":"Often no symptoms in the early stages — damage can be occurring without your knowledge"},{"@type":"MedicalSymptom","name":"Gradually worsening vision"},{"@type":"MedicalSymptom","name":"Floaters (dark spots or strings drifting across your vision)"},{"@type":"MedicalSymptom","name":"Blurred or patchy vision"},{"@type":"MedicalSymptom","name":"Sudden vision loss (in advanced stages due to vitreous haemorrhage or retinal detachment)"},{"@type":"MedicalSymptom","name":"Difficulty seeing in the dark"},{"@type":"MedicalSymptom","name":"Colours appearing washed out"}],"possibleTreatment":{"@type":"MedicalTherapy","description":"In the early stages (background or non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy), the most effective treatment is not an eye treatment at all — it is optimising your diabetes management. Keeping your blood sugar, blood pressure, and cholesterol well controlled can significantly slow or prevent progression"},"riskFactor":[{"@type":"MedicalRiskFactor","name":"Prolonged high blood sugar levels damaging the retinal blood vessels"},{"@type":"MedicalRiskFactor","name":"Long duration of diabetes — the longer you have had diabetes, the higher the risk"},{"@type":"MedicalRiskFactor","name":"Poorly controlled blood glucose (high HbA1c)"},{"@type":"MedicalRiskFactor","name"